("api/search-hint" :names ("search-hint" "(setf search-hint)") :kind :generic-function :kind-name "Generic Function" :package "WXLAMBDA" :summary "Read/write the placeholder independently of the query. Silent writes use shared Input guard; no notify option. No inference from native text or search/cancel events." :position 550627)
("api/select-grid-block" :names ("select-grid-block") :kind :generic-function :kind-name "Generic Function" :package "WXLAMBDA" :summary "Request native block selection with zero-based inclusive bounds." :position 552173)
("api/selection" :names ("selection" "(setf selection)") :kind :generic-function :kind-name "Generic Function" :package "WXLAMBDA" :summary "Read current selection: an index for CHOICE/LIST-BOX/NOTEBOOK, or a canonical managed TREE-ITEM view for TREE-CONTROL, with NIL when no selection exists. Reader NOTIFY is ignored. Writes are silent by default; notifying choice/list writes emit synthetic events, while notebook/tree writes use native vetoable selection. Return of a requested assignment does not prove native acceptance. Default single-tree writes require a live same-tree item; NIL is empty-tree no-op only. For :MULTIPLE-P trees, the reader returns the first selected item and the setter replaces the set with one item or clears it with NIL. TREE-SELECTIONS exposes the complete set. Multi-tree structural edits preserve surviving selections. RADIO-BOX always has exactly one valid index; its explicit notifying write commits before a native-class command event, without veto or rollback. COMBO-BOX selection writes also set item text; NIL clears both text and selection. Arbitrary text writes do not promise a selected item. LIST-CONTROL is fixed single selection, NIL clears; silent by default, :notify T allows actual native selected/deselected events." :position 552927)
("api/set-stc-save-point" :names ("set-stc-save-point") :kind :generic-function :kind-name "Generic Function" :package "WXLAMBDA" :summary "Invoke native SetSavePoint. Arguments use the checked native domains described in the styled-text guide." :position 558343)
